06: 178&#x2013;186.</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">18988763</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Awede B, Thissen JP, Gailly P, Lebacq J (1999) Regulation of IGF-I, IGFBP-4 and IGFBP-5 gene expression by loading in mouse skeletal muscle. FEBS Letters 461: 263&#x2013;267.</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">10567708</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Haddad F, Baldwin KM, Tesch PA (2005) Pretranslational markers of contractile protein expression in human skeletal muscle: effect of limb unloading plus resistance exercise. J Appl Physiol 98: 46&#x2013;52.</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">15298986</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Bigbee AJ, Grindeland RE, Roy RR, Zhong H, Gosselink KL, et al. (2006) Basal and evoked levels of bioassayable growth hormone are altered by hindlimb unloading. J Appl Physiol 100: 1037&#x2013;1042.</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">16339349</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Sonntag WE, Ramsey M, Carter CS (2005) Growth hormone and insulin-like growth factor-1 (IGF-1) and their influence on cognitive aging. Ageing Res Rev 4: 195&#x2013;212.</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">16024298</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Suliman IA, Lindgren JU, Elhassan AM, Diab KM, Adem A (2001) Effects of short- and long-term rat hind limb immobilization on spinal cord insulin-like growth factor-I and its receptor. Brain Res 912: 17&#x2013;23.</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">11520489</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Rotwein P, Burgess SK, Milbrandt JD, Krause JE (1988) Differential expression of insulin-like growth factor genes in rat central nervous system. Proc Natl Acad Sci USA 85: 265&#x2013;269.</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="pmc">PMC279525</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">3422422</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Mitschelen M, Yan H, Farley JA, Warrington JP, Han S, et al. (2011) Long-term deficiency of circulating and hippocampal insulin-like growth factor I induces depressive behavior in adult mice: a potential model of geriatric depression. Neuroscience 185: 50&#x2013;60.</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="pmc">PMC3101268</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">21524689</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Yamamoto H, Murphy LJ (1995) Enzymatic conversion of IGF-I to des(1-3)IGF-I in rat serum and tissues: a further potential site of growth hormone regulation of IGF-I action. J Endocrinol146: 141&#x2013;148.</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">7561610</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Carro E, Nu&#xf1;ez A, Busiguina S, Torres-Aleman I (2000) Circulating insulin-like growth factor I mediates effects of exercise on the brain. J Neurosci 20: 2926&#x2013;2633.</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="pmc">PMC6772191</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">10751445</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Carro E, Spuch C, Trejo JL, Antequera D, Torres-Aleman I (2005) Choroid plexus megalin is involved in neuroprotection by serum insulin-like growth factor I. J Neurosci. 25: 10884&#x2013;10893.</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="pmc">PMC6725866</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">16306401</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Yamaguchi F, Itano T, Mizobuchi M, Miyamoto O, Janjua NA, et al. (1990) Insulin-like growth factor I (IGF-I) distribution in the tissue and extracellular compartment in different regions of rat brain. Brain Res 533: 344&#x2013;347.</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">2289149</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Kar S, Chabot JG, Quirion R (1993) Quantitative autoradiographic localization of [125I]insulin-like growth factor I, [125I]insulin-like growth factor II, and [125I]insulin receptor binding sites in developing and adult rat brain. J Comp Neurol 333: 375&#x2013;397.</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">8349849</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Cullen KE, Roy JE (2004) Signal processing in the vestibular system during active versus passive head movements. J Neurophysiol 91: 1919&#x2013;1933.</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">15069088</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Chung YH, Shin CM, Joo KM, Kim MJ, Cha CI (2002) Age-related upregulation of insulin-like growth factor receptor type I in rat cerebellum. Neurosci Lett 330: 65&#x2013;68.</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">12213636</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Dor&#xe9; S, Kar S, Quirion R (1997) Rediscovering an old friend, IGF-I: potential use in the treatment of neurodegenerative diseases. Trends Neurosci 20: 326&#x2013;331.</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">9246719</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Middleton FA, Strick PL (2000) Basal ganglia and cerebellar loops: motor and cognitive circuits. Brain Res Rev 31: 236&#x2013;250.</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">10719151</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Niblock MM, Brunso-Bechtold JK, Riddle DR (2000) Insulin-like growth factor I stimulates dendritic growth in primary somatosensory cortex. J Neurosci 20: 4165&#x2013;4176.</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="pmc">PMC6772633</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">10818152</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Glasper ER, Llorens-Martin MV, Leuner B, Gould E, Trejo JL (2010) Blockade of insulin-like growth factor-I has complex effects on structural plasticity in the hippocampus. Hippocampus 20: 706&#x2013;712.</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">19603528</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Lee CC, Huang CC, Hsu KS (2011) Insulin promotes dendritic spine and synapse formation by the PI3K/Akt/mTOR and Rac1 signaling pathways. Neuropharmacology 61: 867&#x2013;879.</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">21683721</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Dupont E, Canu MH, Stevens L, Falempin M (2005) Effects of a 14-day period of hindpaw sensory restriction on mRNA and protein levels of NGF and BDNF in the hindpaw primary somatosensory cortex. Mol Brain Res. 133: 78&#x2013;86.</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">15661367</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Kanzaki M, Zhang YQ, Mashima H, Li L, Shibata H, et al. (1999) Translocation of a calcium-permeable cation channel induced by insulin-like growth factor-I. Nat Cell Biol 1: 165&#x2013;170.</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">10559903</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Blair LA, Marshall J (1997) IGF-1 modulates N and L calcium channels in a PI 3-kinase-dependent manner. Neuron 19: 421&#x2013;429.</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">9292730</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Shan H, Messi ML, Zheng Z, Wang ZM, Delbono O (2003) Preservation of motor neuron Ca2+ channel sensitivity to insulin-like growth factor-1 in brain motor cortex from senescent rat. J Physiol 553: 49&#x2013;63.</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="pmc">PMC2343486</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">12963799</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Langlet C, Canu MH, Viltart O, Sequeira H, Falempin M (2001) Hypodynamia &#x2013; hypokinesia induced variations in expression of fos protein in structures related to somatosensory system in the rat. Brain Res 905: 72&#x2013;80.</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">11423081</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Dupont E, Canu MH, Falempin M (2003) A 14-day period of hindpaw sensory deprivation enhances the responsiveness of rat cortical neurons. Neuroscience 121: 433&#x2013;439.</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">14522001</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Nu&#xf1;ez A, Carro E, Torres-Aleman I (2003) Insulin-like growth factor I modifies electrophysiological properties of rat brain stem neurons. J Neurophysiol 89: 3008&#x2013;3017.</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">12612011</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Dupont E, Stevens L, Cochon L, Falempin M, Bastide B, et al. (2011) ERK is involved in the reorganization of somatosensory cortical maps in adult rats submitted to hindlimb unloading. PLoS One. 6(3): e17564.</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="pmc">PMC3050880</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">21408155</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference></ReferenceList></PubmedData></PubmedArticle><PubmedArticle><MedlineCitation Status="MEDLINE" Owner="NLM" IndexingMethod="Manual"><PMID Version="1">25666623</PMID><DateCompleted><Year>2015</Year><Month>06</Month><Day>26</Day></DateCompleted><DateRevised><Year>2021</Year><Month>02</Month><Day>05</Day></DateRevised><Article PubModel="Print-Electronic"><Journal><ISSN IssnType="Electronic">1083-351X</ISSN><JournalIssue CitedMedium="Internet"><Volume>290</Volume><Issue>15</Issue><PubDate><Year>2015</Year><Month>Apr</Month><Day>10</Day></PubDate></JournalIssue><Title>The Journal of biological chemistry</Title><ISOAbbreviation>J Biol Chem</ISOAbbreviation></Journal><ArticleTitle>LINGO-1 protein interacts with the p75 neurotrophin receptor in intracellular membrane compartments.</ArticleTitle><Pagination><StartPage>9511</StartPage><EndPage>9520</EndPage><MedlinePgn>9511-20</MedlinePgn></Pagination><ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1074/jbc.M114.608018</ELocationID><Abstract><AbstractText>Axon outgrowth inhibition in response to trauma is thought to be mediated via the binding of myelin-associated inhibitory factors (e.g. Nogo-66, myelin-associated glycoprotein, oligodendrocyte myelin glycoprotein, and myelin basic protein) to a putative tripartite LINGO-1&#xb7;p75(NTR)&#xb7;Nogo-66 receptor (NgR) complex at the cell surface. We found that endogenous LINGO-1 expression in neurons in the cortex and cerebellum is intracellular. Mutation or truncation of the highly conserved LINGO-1 C terminus altered this intracellular localization, causing poor intracellular retention and increased plasma membrane expression. p75(NTR) associated predominantly with natively expressed LINGO-1 containing immature N-glycans, characteristic of protein that has not completed trans-Golgi-mediated processing, whereas mutant forms of LINGO-1 with enhanced plasma membrane expression did not associate with p75(NTR). Co-immunoprecipitation experiments demonstrated that LINGO-1 and NgR competed for binding to p75(NTR) in a manner that is difficult to reconcile with the existence of a LINGO-1&#xb7;p75(NTR)&#xb7;NgR ternary complex. These findings contradict models postulating functional LINGO-1&#xb7;p75(NTR)&#xb7;NgR complexes in the plasma membrane.</AbstractText><CopyrightInformation>&#xa9; 2015 by The American Society for Biochemistry and Molecular Biology, Inc.</CopyrightInformation></Abstract><AuthorList CompleteYN="Y"><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Meabon</LastName><ForeName>James S</ForeName><Initials>JS</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>From the Departments of Psychiatry and Behavioral Sciences, the Mental Illness Research Education and Clinical Center, Veterans Affairs Medical Center, Seattle, Washington 98108.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>De Laat</LastName><ForeName>Rian</ForeName><Initials>R</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Immusoft, Seattle, Washington 98103, and.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Ieguchi</LastName><ForeName>Katsuaki</ForeName><Initials>K</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>the Department of Pharmacology, Tokyo Women's Medical University, Tokyo 162-8666, Japan.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Wiley</LastName><ForeName>Jesse C</ForeName><Initials>JC</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Comparative Medicine, and.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Hudson</LastName><ForeName>Mark P</ForeName><Initials>MP</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Physiology and Biophysics, University of Washington, Seattle, Washington 98195.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Bothwell</LastName><ForeName>Mark</ForeName><Initials>M</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Physiology and Biophysics, University of Washington, Seattle, Washington 98195, mab@uw.edu.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author></AuthorList><Language>eng</Language><GrantList CompleteYN="Y"><Grant><GrantID>R01 NS047348</GrantID><Acronym>NS</Acronym><Agency>NINDS NIH HHS</Agency><Country>United States</Country></Grant><Grant><GrantID>NS47348</GrantID><Acronym>NS</Acronym><Agency>NINDS NIH HHS</Agency><Country>United States</Country></Grant></GrantList><PublicationTypeList><PublicationType UI="D016428">Journal Article</PublicationType><PublicationType UI="D052061">Research Support, N.I.H., Extramural</PublicationType></PublicationTypeList><ArticleDate DateType="Electronic"><Year>2015</Year><Month>02</Month><Day>09</Day></ArticleDate></Article><MedlineJournalInfo><Country>United States</Country><MedlineTA>J Biol Chem</MedlineTA><NlmUniqueID>2985121R</NlmUniqueID><ISSNLinking>0021-9258</ISSNLinking></MedlineJournalInfo><ChemicalList><Chemical><RegistryNumber>0</RegistryNumber><NameOfSubstance UI="D058851">GPI-Linked Proteins</NameOfSubstance></Chemical><Chemical><RegistryNumber>0</RegistryNumber><NameOfSubstance UI="C482293">LINGO1 protein, human</NameOfSubstance></Chemical><Chemical><RegistryNumber>0</RegistryNumber><NameOfSubstance UI="D008565">Membrane Proteins</NameOfSubstance></Chemical><Chemical><RegistryNumber>0</RegistryNumber><NameOfSubstance UI="D009185">Myelin Proteins</NameOfSubstance></Chemical><Chemical><RegistryNumber>0</RegistryNumber><NameOfSubstance UI="C484065">NGFR protein, human</NameOfSubstance></Chemical><Chemical><RegistryNumber>0</RegistryNumber><NameOfSubstance UI="D009419">Nerve Tissue Proteins</NameOfSubstance></Chemical><Chemical><RegistryNumber>0</RegistryNumber><NameOfSubstance UI="D000070816">Nogo Receptor 1</NameOfSubstance></Chemical><Chemical><RegistryNumber>0</RegistryNumber><NameOfSubstance UI="D011134">Polysaccharides</NameOfSubstance></Chemical><Chemical><RegistryNumber>0</RegistryNumber><NameOfSubstance UI="C486580">RTN4R protein, human</NameOfSubstance></Chemical><Chemical><RegistryNumber>0</RegistryNumber><NameOfSubstance UI="D011956">Receptors, Cell Surface</NameOfSubstance></Chemical><Chemical><RegistryNumber>0</RegistryNumber><NameOfSubstance UI="D017475">Receptors, Nerve Growth Factor</NameOfSubstance></Chemical></ChemicalList><CitationSubset>IM</CitationSubset><MeshHeadingList><MeshHeading><DescriptorName UI="D000818" MajorTopicYN="N">Animals</DescriptorName></MeshHeading><MeshHeading><DescriptorName UI="D000831" MajorTopicYN="N">Animals, Newborn</DescriptorName></MeshHeading><MeshHeading><DescriptorName UI="D001667" MajorTopicYN="N">Binding, Competitive</DescriptorName></MeshHeading><MeshHeading><DescriptorName UI="D001921" MajorTopicYN="N">Brain</DescriptorName><QualifierName UI="Q000166" MajorTopicYN="N">cytology</QualifierName><QualifierName UI="Q000378" MajorTopicYN="N">metabolism</QualifierName></MeshHeading><MeshHeading><DescriptorName UI="D002462" MajorTopicYN="N">Cell Membrane</DescriptorName><QualifierName UI="Q000378" MajorTopicYN="N">metabolism</QualifierName></MeshHeading><MeshHeading><DescriptorName UI="D058851" MajorTopicYN="N">GPI-Linked Proteins</DescriptorName><QualifierName UI="Q000235" MajorTopicYN="N">genetics</QualifierName><QualifierName UI="Q0