" MajorTopicYN="N">pathology</QualifierName></MeshHeading><MeshHeading><DescriptorName UI="D018920" MajorTopicYN="N">Coculture Techniques</DescriptorName></MeshHeading><MeshHeading><DescriptorName UI="D004353" MajorTopicYN="N">Drug Evaluation, Preclinical</DescriptorName></MeshHeading><MeshHeading><DescriptorName UI="D057026" MajorTopicYN="Y">Induced Pluripotent Stem Cells</DescriptorName></MeshHeading><MeshHeading><DescriptorName UI="D005910" MajorTopicYN="Y">Glioma</DescriptorName><QualifierName UI="Q000473" MajorTopicYN="N">pathology</QualifierName></MeshHeading><MeshHeading><DescriptorName UI="D009940" MajorTopicYN="N">Organoids</DescriptorName></MeshHeading><MeshHeading><DescriptorName UI="D001932" MajorTopicYN="Y">Brain Neoplasms</DescriptorName></MeshHeading><MeshHeading><DescriptorName UI="D016548" MajorTopicYN="N">Prosencephalon</DescriptorName></MeshHeading><MeshHeading><DescriptorName UI="D002454" MajorTopicYN="N">Cell Differentiation</DescriptorName></MeshHeading><MeshHeading><DescriptorName UI="D002528" MajorTopicYN="Y">Cerebellar Neoplasms</DescriptorName><QualifierName UI="Q000473" MajorTopicYN="N">pathology</QualifierName></MeshHeading></MeshHeadingList></MedlineCitation><PubmedData><History><PubMedPubDate PubStatus="received"><Year>2022</Year><Month>10</Month><Day>11</Day></PubMedPubDate><PubMedPubDate PubStatus="accepted"><Year>2023</Year><Month>3</Month><Day>27</Day></PubMedPubDate><PubMedPubDate PubStatus="medline"><Year>2023</Year><Month>7</Month><Day>12</Day><Hour>6</Hour><Minute>42</Minute></PubMedPubDate><PubMedPubDate PubStatus="pubmed"><Year>2023</Year><Month>5</Month><Day>30</Day><Hour>1</Hour><Minute>6</Minute></PubMedPubDate><PubMedPubDate PubStatus="entrez"><Year>2023</Year><Month>5</Month><Day>29</Day><Hour>23</Hour><Minute>25</Minute></PubMedPubDate></History><PublicationStatus>ppublish</PublicationStatus><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">37248391</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="doi">10.1038/s41596-023-00839-2</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="pii">10.1038/s41596-023-00839-2</ArticleId></ArticleIdList><ReferenceList><Reference><Citation>Antonica, F. et al. Modeling brain tumors: a perspective overview of in vivo and organoid models. Front. Mol. Neurosci. 15, 818696 (2022).</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="doi">10.3389/fnmol.2022.818696</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">35706426</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="pmc">9190727</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Bian, S. et al. Genetically engineered cerebral organoids model brain tumor formation. Nat. Methods 15, 631&#x2013;639 (2018).</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="doi">10.1038/s41592-018-0070-7</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">30038414</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="pmc">6071863</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Ogawa, J., Pao, G. M., Shokhirev, M. N. &amp; Verma, I. M. Glioblastoma model using human cerebral organoids. Cell Rep. 23, 1220&#x2013;1229 (2018).</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="doi">10.1016/j.celrep.2018.03.105</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">29694897</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="pmc">6892608</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Antonica, F. et al. A slow-cycling/quiescent cells subpopulation is involved in glioma invasiveness. Nat. Commun. 13, 4767 (2022).</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="doi">10.1038/s41467-022-32448-0</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">35970913</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="pmc">9378633</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Ballabio, C. et al. Modeling medulloblastoma in vivo and with human cerebellar organoids. Nat. Commun. 11, 583 (2020).</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="doi">10.1038/s41467-019-13989-3</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">31996670</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="pmc">6989674</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Ballabio, C. et al. Notch1 switches progenitor competence in inducing medulloblastoma. Sci. Adv. 7, eabd2781 (2021).</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="doi">10.1126/sciadv.abd2781</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">34162555</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="pmc">8221631</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Muguruma, K., Nishiyama, A., Kawakami, H., Hashimoto, K. &amp; Sasai, Y. Self-organization of polarized cerebellar tissue in 3D culture of human pluripotent stem cells. Cell Rep. 10, 537&#x2013;550 (2015).</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="doi">10.1016/j.celrep.2014.12.051</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">25640179</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Velasco, S. et al. Individual brain organoids reproducibly form cell diversity of the human cerebral cortex. Nature https://doi.org/10.1038/s41586-019-1289-x (2019).</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="doi">10.1038/s41586-019-1289-x</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">31168097</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="pmc">6906116</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Lancaster, M. A. et al. Cerebral organoids model human brain development and microcephaly. Nature 501, 373&#x2013;379 (2013).</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="doi">10.1038/nature12517</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">23995685</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="pmc">3817409</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Kadoshima, T. et al. Self-organization of axial polarity, inside-out layer pattern, and species-specific progenitor dynamics in human ES cell-derived neocortex. Proc. Natl Acad. Sci. USA 110, 20284&#x2013;20289 (2013).</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="doi">10.1073/pnas.1315710110</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">24277810</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="pmc">3864329</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Li, X. et al. PiggyBac transposase tools for genome engineering. Proc. Natl Acad. Sci. USA 110, E2279&#x2013;E2287 (2013).</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">23723351</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="pmc">3690869</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Zhang, T. et al. Generation of excitatory and inhibitory neurons from common progenitors via Notch signaling in the cerebellum. Cell Rep. 35, 109208 (2021).</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="doi">10.1016/j.celrep.2021.109208</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">34107249</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Oki, T. et al. A novel cell-cycle-indicator, mVenus-p27K-, identifies quiescent cells and visualizes G0-G1 transition. Sci. Rep. 4, 4012 (2014).</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="doi">10.1038/srep04012</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">24500246</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="pmc">3915272</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Pan, Y. et al. Athymic mice reveal a requirement for T-cell-microglia interactions in establishing a microenvironment supportive of Nf1 low-grade glioma growth. Genes Dev. 32, 491&#x2013;496 (2018).</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="doi">10.1101/gad.310797.117</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">29632086</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="pmc">5959233</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Mathon, B. et al. Increasing the effectiveness of intracerebral injections in adult and neonatal mice: a neurosurgical point of view. Neurosci. Bull. 31, 685&#x2013;696 (2015).</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="doi">10.1007/s12264-015-1558-0</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">26345180</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="pmc">5563725</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Ishida, Y. et al. Erratum: vulnerability of purkinje cells generated from spinocerebellar ataxia type 6 patient-derived iPSCs. Cell Rep. 17, 1482&#x2013;1490 (2017).</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="doi">10.1016/j.celrep.2016.10.026</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference></ReferenceList></PubmedData></PubmedArticle><PubmedArticle><MedlineCitation Status="MEDLINE" Owner="NLM" IndexingMethod="Manual"><PMID Version="1">30636067</PMID><DateCompleted><Year>2020</Year><Month>08</Month><Day>31</Day></DateCompleted><DateRevised><Year>2020</Year><Month>08</Month><Day>31</Day></DateRevised><Article PubModel="Print-Electronic"><Journal><ISSN IssnType="Electronic">1365-2990</ISSN><JournalIssue CitedMedium="Internet"><Volume>45</Volume><Issue>6</Issue><PubDate><Year>2019</Year><Month>Oct</Month></PubDate></JournalIssue><Title>Neuropathology and applied neurobiology</Title><ISOAbbreviation>Neuropathol Appl Neurobiol</ISOAbbreviation></Journal><ArticleTitle>Short Review: Investigating ARSACS: models for understanding cerebellar degeneration.</ArticleTitle><Pagination><StartPage>531</StartPage><EndPage>537</EndPage><MedlinePgn>531-537</MedlinePgn></Pagination><ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1111/nan.12540</ELocationID><Abstract><AbstractText>Autosomal recessive spastic ataxia of Charlevoix-Saguenay (ARSACS) is an early-onset neurodegenerative disease that includes progressive cerebellar dysfunction. ARSACS is caused by an autosomal recessive loss-of-function mutation in the SACS gene, which encodes for SACSIN. Although animal models are still necessary to investigate the role of SACSIN in the pathology of this disease, more reliable human cellular models need to be generated to better understand the cerebellar pathophysiology of ARSACS. The discovery of human induced pluripotent stem cells (hiPSC) has permitted the derivation of patient-specific cells. These cells have an unlimited self-renewing capacity and the ability to differentiate into different neural cell types, allowing studies of disease mechanism, drug discovery and cell replacement therapies. In this study, we discuss how the hiPSC-derived cerebellar organoid culture offers novel strategies for targeting the pathogenic mutations related to ARSACS. We also highlight the advantages and challenges of this 3D cellular model, as well as the questions that still remain unanswered.</AbstractText><CopyrightInformation>&#xa9; 2019 British Neuropathological Society.</CopyrightInformation></Abstract><AuthorList CompleteYN="Y"><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Artero Castro</LastName><ForeName>A</ForeName><Initials>A</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Stem Cells Therapies in Neurodegenerative Diseases Lab, Research Center "Principe Felipe", Valencia, Spain.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Machuca</LastName><ForeName>C</ForeName><Initials>C</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Stem Cells Therapies in Neurodegenerative Diseases Lab, Research Center "Principe Felipe", Valencia, Spain.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Unit of Genetics and Genomics of Neuromuscular and Neurodegenerative Disorders and Service of Genomics and Translational Genetics, Research Center "Principe Felipe", Valencia, Spain.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Rodriguez Jimenez</LastName><ForeName>F J</ForeName><Initials>FJ</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Stem Cells Therapies in Neurodegenerative Diseases Lab, Research Center "Principe Felipe", Valencia, Spain.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Jendelova</LastName><ForeName>P</ForeName><Initials>P</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Institute of Experimental Medicine, Department of Neuroscience, Academy of Science of the Czech Republic, Prague, Czech Republic.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Erceg</LastName><ForeName>S</ForeName><Initials>S</Initials><Identifier Source="ORCID">0000-0003-3208-7705</Identifier><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Stem Cells Therapies in Neurodegenerative Diseases Lab, Research Center "Principe Felipe", Valencia, Spain.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>National Stem Cell Bank-Valencia Node, Platform for Proteomics, Genotyping and Cell Lines, PRB3, ISCIII, Research Center "Principe Fel